{"name":"Pediment","hex":"#d3ccc4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pediment","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A classical architectural element consisting of a triangular section or gable found above the horizontal superstructure (entablature) which lies immediately upon the columns; fronton.","A very gently sloping (0.5°-7°) inclined bedrock surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pediment"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pediment","blocks":[{"text":"Pediments are a form of gable in classical architecture, usually of a triangular shape. Pediments are placed above the horizontal structure of the cornice, or entablature if supported by columns. In ancient architecture, a wide and low triangular pediment typically formed the top element of the portico of a Greek temple, a style continued in Roman temples. Large pediments were rare on other types of building before Renaissance architecture. For symmetric designs, a pediment forms a center point and is often used to add grandness to entrances."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Illustrations_of_types_of_pediments.jpg/330px-Illustrations_of_types_of_pediments.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pediment","wikidata":"Q190996"},{"title":"Pediment (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"A pediment, also known as a concave slope or waning slope, is a very gently sloping (0.5°–7°) inclined bedrock surface. It is typically a concave surface sloping down from the base of a steeper retreating desert cliff, escarpment, or surrounding a monadnock or inselberg, but may persist after the higher terrain has eroded away."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Utah_pediment.jpg/330px-Utah_pediment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pediment_(geology)","wikidata":"Q1048450"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Madeleine_collage.jpg/500px-Madeleine_collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Madeleine_collage.jpg/1280px-Madeleine_collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Madeleine_collage.jpg/500px-Madeleine_collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Neoclassical pediment of the Madeleine Church, Paris, with sculpture (1826–1834) by Philippe Joseph Henri Lemaire[3]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMadeleine_collage.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Palazzo_Farnese_Roma_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-Palazzo_Farnese_Roma_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Palazzo_Farnese_Roma_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-Palazzo_Farnese_Roma_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Open pediments on windows at the Palazzo Farnese, Rome, by Antonio da Sangallo the Younger, begun 1534","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APalazzo_Farnese_Roma_(cropped).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Roman_fresco_from_Boscoreale%2C_43-30_BCE%2C_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg/250px-Roman_fresco_from_Boscoreale%2C_43-30_BCE%2C_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Roman_fresco_from_Boscoreale%2C_43-30_BCE%2C_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg/330px-Roman_fresco_from_Boscoreale%2C_43-30_BCE%2C_Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wik